.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Huh
and
.I Bits
color the text of the
.IR acme (1)
window
.B $winid
by what a language model makes of it: how many bits each token costs, from
.IR lmfs ,
or
.IR lmsim (4)
in tests, mounted on
.I lmdir
.RB ( /mnt/lm ).
If
.I lmdir
has no
.BR clone ,
it mounts
.B /srv/lm
there, as
.B lmsim
.B -s
.B lm
posts it, since acme runs a command in acme's own namespace.
Each is meant to be run from the window's tag.
Each follows the window by its
.BR rdevent ,
so it leaves the window's
.B event
to acme or another program, and both can follow one window.
One that starts on a window whose
.I astyles
layers already include its own deletes that layer and its own, and exits; the program whose
layer is gone sees so within a quarter second and ends.
So the same word in the tag turns it on and off.
.I Huh
highlights the stretches where the text's information is, those that cost the most;
.I Bits
colors every segment of the text in one of seven bands of bits a rune, and draws the text as a strip in a
window of its own.
.PP
Each writes a layer of
.IR astyles (4)
on the window,
.B huh
or
.BR bits .
It makes the layer, opens the window's
.B rdevent
file from the window's
.BR seq ,
and then reads its body under the same
.BR seq ,
so that no edit falls between.
It writes the text to a session of
.IR lmdir 's
.B data
file, after a preamble, the window's name as its tag has it, a colon and a blank line, as
.IR bits (1)
writes a file's, and leaves out the answer's lines for the preamble; a window with no name gets
none.
It marks the tokens it gets back and writes the layer;
.I Bits
splits them into segments first, as
.IR segment (1)
does.
Then it follows the window's edits, by the
.B I
and
.B D
events, in a copy of the body: after each one it writes the whole copy to
.B data
on a new open and closes the old one, which drops what was left of it, marks again, and writes the layer again, under
.IR astyles 's
.B seq
guard, so a layer from an older text is not kept.
.PP
.I Huh
highlights, in the color
.BR huh ,
the stretches that
.I huh
prints, below, at its defaults, and segments nothing.
What it lights is where the model spends its bits: names, terms, code and figures, more than a
sentence that is merely new to the text.
It is meant for prose; code mostly falls under the floor, below.
Once a text is first scored and has been idle for a second,
.I Huh
holds its cutoffs and remembers its highlights.
After an edit, a run of tokens above the leaving cutoff stays highlighted while it overlaps a
highlight from before, and a new one enters only with a token above the cutoff for 0.7 times
the density, so a highlight away from the edit stays as it was.
When the text has again been idle for a second with the whole answer in,
.I Huh
fits the cutoffs again, and highlights afresh as
.I huh
does, only when the runes above the held cutoff differ from the density's share by more than
10% of it and by more than 128 runes.
While a text is first scored, the cutoffs come from the tokens scored so far;
Get, a new text and a change of density start afresh.
.PP
The density, in percent, from 5 to 30, sets the cutoff a highlight enters by, 10 by default; with
the leaving cutoff and whole words, the highlights cover a little more of a dense text, about 13%
of the tree's docs, and less of a bland one.
.B $home/lib/huh
holds the user's choice, a line
.BI density " n"\f1,
over the default.
.I Huh+
and
.IR Huh- ,
links to
.IR Huh ,
raise and lower it by 5, write
.BR $home/lib/huh
and print the new density,
as
.I Huh
with a
.I density
does, swept in the tag as
.BR "Huh 15" ;
they need no window.
A running
.I Huh
reads the file again when it changes, and marks again by the new density at once.
.PP
The colors are tints the programs give in their layers' own color lines, each with an opacity,
so that
.IR astyles (4)
blends them over the text's other colors in either theme.
.IR Bits '
.I penalty
and
.I runes
are
.IR segment 's
.B -p
and
.BR -l .
.PP
.I Bits
colors each segment in its color,
.B bits0
to
.BR bits6 ,
by its bits a rune: under 0.5, 0.5 to 1, 1 to 2, 2 to 3, 3 to 4.5, 4.5 to 6, and 6 or more.
It also opens a drawn window, named for the file with
.BR +Bits ,
that shows the text as a strip, a row for each of its lines and a block for each word, in its
band's color, and beside it a bar for each paragraph, from
.B bits
.BR -p :
its length the paragraph's bits alone, and its dark part those the text before it already had.
Button 1 on the strip selects its token in the text's window and shows it;
button 1 on a bar, its paragraph's start.
Button 3 on the strip writes the token's alternatives, as
.I Huh
.B -t
does, below.
.PP
The program reaches the window's files through
.IR acme (2).
Acme handles every event in the text's window itself; the program only watches them.
After a
.BR Get ,
it reads the reloaded body and colors it again.
When the window is deleted its
.B rdevent
ends, and so does the program.
However the program ends, by the window's deletion, a failure of
.IR lmdir ,
its layer's deletion or a note, it deletes its layer and its own window.
Del or
.B Bits
in
.IR Bits 's
own window ends it too.
.PP
.B Huh
.B -t
writes the alternatives for the token at the window's dot to
.B +Errors
and exits, in any window, with or without
.I Huh
or
.IR Bits ;
.B cmd-k
.B h
in
.B /lib/hotkey/acme
runs it on the window with the focus.
The line gives the token's address in the file, the token, its bits and rank, and the model's
five likeliest tokens in its place, from
.IR lmdir 's
.BR top ,
each with its bits; button 3 on the line shows the token.
The alternatives tell a misspelling, whose likeliest token is the right spelling, from a new
idea.
.PP
The command
.I huh
scores each
.IR file ,
or its standard input, once, and prints a line for each highlighted stretch:
.IP
.EX
README.md:#2210,#2398 3.41
.EE
.LP
its address and its score.
A file goes to the model after its preamble, as in
.IR bits (1),
and its addresses are
.IR file :# q0 ,# q1 .
Standard input has no name, so it gets no preamble and its addresses are
.RI # q0 ,# q1
alone;
.B -n
gives it
.I name
for both.
.PP
Each token's bits are clipped at
.I bits
(15 by default) and spread over its runes, less, in a text's first 512 tokens, a position
baseline: the bits a rune the first tokens of the tree's docs cost beyond those later in them,
since a text's start costs more for lack of context even after its preamble.
Each token's score is the clipped bits over the runes of a window of
.I width
tokens around it (32 by default), weighted by a triangle that falls off from the token.
The cutoff is the score above which the tokens cover
.I density
percent of the text's runes, 10 by default; unlike
.IR Huh ,
.I huh
reads no file.
The cutoffs stop at a floor of 1.7 bits a rune, near the 90th percentile of the scores over the
tree's docs, man pages and C, so a bland text gets less than its share, or nothing, and a text
under twice
.I width
tokens gets nothing.
A stretch is a run of tokens above the cutoff for 1.5 times the density that holds a token above
the cutoff for the density, and is at least
.I tokens
tokens long (4 by default);
.B -l
.B 0
keeps every one.
A stretch's ends move out to the blanks around the words they fall in, up to 32 runes, so a
highlight holds whole words, and stretches that then meet are one.
A stretch's score is its tokens' scores, weighted by their runes.
With
.BR -a ,
.I huh
prints every stretch, a run of tokens above the median score, highlighted or not.
Nothing is parsed: the text is plain text to the model, and every stretch comes from the tokens'
bits and their places.
